THE GATE. Two tracked files named the operator's home directory and both had
reached main. Neither was careless: both sat inside measured evidence, which is
the trap CLAUDE.md names out loud. The rule had no script, so it relied on
remembering and was forgotten twice.

scripts/no-operator-paths.sh fails on an absolute path under a user home
directory in any git-tracked file outside docs/, which is local-only. It allows
/home/innsegl, the container image's own user — a gate that called both
Dockerfiles violations would be switched off within a week.

The selftest was observed failing first, against a gate that did not exist.
Then it was mutation-tested, and that is what earned the sixth case: with the
allowlist deleted the selftest still reported five passes, because case 4 had
planted  — which the pattern never matches, so the case had
never once reached the allowlist it existed to test. It now plants a path with a
trailing segment, and a fifth case proves the allowlist matches the container's
user rather than merely starting with it. The selftest plants its own violations
and assembles the literals from fragments rather than claiming an exemption: a
gate with an exemption is a gate with a place to hide.

